{
  "data": {
    "text": "Spontaneous eye movements during eyes-open rest reduce resting-state-network modularity by increasing visual-sensorimotor connectivity\n\nCemal Koba, Giuseppe Notaro, Sandra Tamm, Gustav Nilsonne, and Uri Hasson\n\nNetwork Neuroscience, 5(2), 451–476. https://doi.org/10.1162/netn_a_00186. The MIT Press. 2021.\n\nan open access journal • Published under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0) license.\n\nWe used eyes-open resting-state (RS) fMRI with simultaneous eye tracking and found that removing eye-orbit EPI variance reduced connectivity between sensorimotor and visual areas and increased network modularity.\n\nAll data are available online from OpenNeuro, Dataset ds000201 (https://www.openneuro.org/datasets/ds000201/). We include the analysis workflow as Supporting Information, also available via a GitHub repository (Koba, 2021).\n\nData were collected from 86 participants on a 3T MRI scanner (Discovery 750, General Electric) using an 8-channel head coil. For the resting state EPI images: slice thickness 3 mm no gap, axial orientation, 49 slices covering the entire brain, interleaved acquisition inferior to superior, TE = 30, TR = 2.5 sec, flip angle 75°. One RS run per participant was a typical RS scan acquired in absence of sleep deprivation, where participants were asked to fixate on a white cross presented on a gray background for 8 minutes. Three participants did not provide these runs so 83 participants were included in our initial sample. Participants belonged to two age groups: 20–30 years of age (n = 45, Median = 23) and 65–75 years of age (n = 38, Median = 68).\n\nFirst, we applied brain extraction and tissue segmentation (gray matter, white matter, CSF) to the structural T1 images using the antsBrainExtraction function of ANTs software. We registered each participant’s structural image to standard space using non-linear registration (ICBM 2009 nonlinear asymetric template) and saved the inverse of the warps. We also registered the structural and functional images using affine transformation. We also created cerebral spinal fluid (CSF) and white matter masks in MNI space and transformed them to individual space, where they were eroded by one voxel from their outer boundaries to be more conservative. We then extracted the mean time series from these white matter and CSF masks.\n\nWe used AFNI for analyzing the functional RS images. We implemented the following steps: slice time correction, motion correction (base image: first volume of the run), and band-pass filtering (0.01–0.1 Hz). To remove other nuisance sources of variance from the functional time series, we implemented preliminary data-cleaning using regression with the following regressors: (a) motion parameters estimated during motion correction, (b) mean white matter and CSF time series, and (c) frame-wise displacement values. To improve signal to noise of the subsequent regression models that were of primary interest, we then spatially smoothed the cleaned time series with a 6-mm FWHM kernel."
  },
